<p>Paris prosecutor Laure Beccuau said about 10 suspected victims previously unknown to investigators have come forward in France’s investigation into Jeffrey Epstein’s network, bringing to about 20 the number of people who contacted prosecutors after her February appeal for witnesses. French magistrates opened an investigation into human trafficking after the U.S. Justice Department released another cache of Epstein files in January. Investigators are examining possible offences committed in France or involving French nationals who may have facilitated Epstein’s crimes. Beccuau told RTL that investigators’ immediate step was to hear from the suspected victims, some of whom live abroad, while scheduling meetings around their ability to travel to Paris. She said investigators had not yet questioned potential suspects and would interview them once they had a fuller picture of Epstein’s relations with others in the French network. Investigators are reviewing the Epstein files for names mentioned by alleged victims and have again retrieved Epstein’s computer, telephone records and address books, Beccuau said. French investigators searched Epstein’s luxury Paris apartment in September 2019, after he was found dead in his New York jail cell in August 2019 while awaiting trial on sex-trafficking charges.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/mxs1p8uuE9nl9q76zd2pqMg_v05zt7vgikWZF3GIHTqNz_faEAeC_2Sfz3A5Q7Fu0j63NumFKgGFnsqhxaYaJl-pxa6BucTspwgEjjblw8cd' alt='Paris public prosecutor Laure Beccuau at the Tribunal de Paris in March 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Known witnesses: Some suspected victims were already known to investigators through inquiries involving former European model agency boss Gerald Marie and the late model agent Jean-Luc Brunel.</li><li>Marie inquiry: Fifteen women urged French authorities in March to investigate Gerald Marie for possible wrongdoing, Dawn reported.</li><li>Facilitation example: Investigators are seeking to identify people who m